    "summary": "Epic Alligator → Caution jam with haunting Lovelight and strong second half",
    "blurb": "The second half shines with an extended Alligator → Caution → Feedback marathon and a soulful Turn On Your Lovelight. Jerry’s guitar tone and Tom Constanten’s organ are front‑and‑center, while Phil lays down solid bass foundations. Recording quality improves midway, delivering a clear portrait of the late‑'69 sound.",
    "review": "December 12, 1969 at Thelma captured the Dead in a transitional moment – Tom Constanten was on his way out, and the band leaned into the swirling organ textures that would define their early ’70s explorations. The first half is a mixed bag of blues‑rock staples, but once the set moves past “Uncle John’s Band,” the energy lifts. A monumental Alligator → Caution → Feedback sequence stretches beyond thirty minutes, showcasing Jerry’s soaring lead lines, Phil’s thunderous “bombs” on bass, and an unrelenting drum partnership that tightens as the jam unfolds. The highlight of the night is a haunting rendition of “Turn On Your Lovelight,” where Tom’s organ weaves a counter‑harmony around Garcia’s guitar, creating a moment that feels both intimate and transcendent. Overall, the show delivers a powerful second half that stands out among late‑’69 recordings.",
